ULTRARAG ATTRIBUTION

memory-rag serves UltraRAG's memory: one standing document per scope, kept in a SQLite
record with a word index and vectors, and reached through the tools record_memory,
recall_memory, forget_memory, and record_handoff.

The behaviour, the file names, and the file formats are UltraRAG's, taken from the
upstream repository at a pinned revision:

  Upstream repository: https://github.com/OpenBMB/UltraRAG
  Upstream revision:   3a709a2aea3fbe46acca59c422621c94b6e86857
  Declared version:    0.3.0.2
  Reference file:      servers/memory/src/memory.py
  Upstream license:    Apache License 2.0

No UltraRAG source file is copied, vendored, or redistributed by this project. The
behaviour and the formats were reimplemented in original Python from that reference, and
this project's output is checked against files the upstream server at that revision
produced, which are committed under tests/fixtures/upstream/ with the method used to
capture them.

THE DIFFERENCE THIS PROJECT MAKES

The record is the same and the four memory tools are the same. What changed is where
the memory is managed from: one process now holds the account's global memory and every
project's local memory at once, and serves a browser workspace, an agent surface, and a
command line from one loopback port. An agent's surface is unchanged — the same four
tools, with the same arguments and the same answers — because the tools are what an
agent should have and the rest is a person's work.

The workspace and the command line add a SQL panel, which reads the record without
restriction and writes only the `unit` table. An accepted write re-renders the standing
document, drops the vectors of the statements it changed, and queues them again, so a
hand edit cannot leave a memory whose words and whose vectors disagree.

The account's settings directory, the environment variable prefix, the model cache,
and the scope directory names keep the names the previous product used, and the
on-disk contract is frozen while that product is installed, because both read and write
the same files.

LOCAL MODEL AND LIBRARY ATTRIBUTION

A lookup runs a local sentence embedder on the CPU, and the search uses a word index.
The weights are fetched once from the model host into this project's own cache and then
run in this process. No API is used at any point, and no data leaves the machine.

  Embedding model:  BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5 (default; others are listed in
                    src/memory_rag/models.py)
  Model license:    MIT (BAAI)
  Rerankers:        jinaai/jina-reranker-v2-base-multilingual and others, listed in
                    the same file
  Run-time library: fastembed (Apache License 2.0), embedding via ONNX Runtime (MIT)
  Arithmetic:       numpy (BSD 3-Clause)
  Word index:       SQLite FTS5, part of the Python standard library's sqlite3 module

The models are data, not source: none of their code is copied or redistributed here, and
each carries its own licence.

THE BROWSER WORKSPACE

The workspace is built on ui-ultra-rag-mcp, an independent companion project that
provides reusable local UI infrastructure for UltraRAG-derived MCP servers, pinned here
by commit. It is not part of UltraRAG and carries no UltraRAG code. This project
supplies a thin adapter and a profile; no part of the workspace is copied in, and this
project declares the corpus capabilities off because it serves memories rather than
documents.

The settings layer is built on config-ultra-rag-mcp, an independent companion project,
likewise pinned by commit. Neither companion project reads this project's storage, and
this project reads neither of theirs.

